{
 "id": "2025-liu-rhododendron",
 "name": "Rhododendron Forest Scenic Beauty from Drone Photos (SBE rating)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Liu, Liao, Huang, Li, Wu, Yi, Wang & Chen (2025), Horticulturae 11:898. The 16 photographs are nadir drone shots taken 100 m above flowering Rhododendron simsii forest in DaWei Mountain National Forest Park, Hunan, China. Participants first preview all 16 together and answer six background questions (gender, age, education, familiarity with the park, forestry/landscape background, familiarity with SBE). They then see each photograph on its own, in a fixed order (Sample Plot 1–16) for 60 seconds, and rate it on the 7-point Scenic Beauty Estimation scale (very like 3 … very dislike −3). Consent text, instructions, questions and answer options follow the paper's Supplementary File S1. In the original online survey (1,549 participants), plots with large, connected red flower patches scored highest, and the share of red flower patches was the strongest predictor of scenic beauty.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) English only; the original was in Chinese. The consent text is a shortened version of the English one in File S1. (2) The 60-second display is enforced: the Next button unlocks after 60 s and a rating is required. The paper says only \"fixed display duration (60 s per image)\". (3) Each trial also records the landscape unit from the paper's Table 4 (BSH/BSL/JBI/JBII 1–4). This label was inferred by matching the rating counts in File S1 to the Table 4 means; it is not stated in the paper.\n\nPhotographs: Liu et al. (2025), Supplementary File S1 (CC BY 4.0). Used with credit for research; the template is removed if the authors object.",
